          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Cordelia’s love for Lear as a present emotional and relational state. Its qualification limits the claimed degree of love rather than weakening its truth-presenting form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Cordelia weakly invokes an existing filial obligation by defining her love according to her bond. She does not make a new promise in the focal expression; rather, she presents herself as loving Lear within the duties already binding daughter to father.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The utterance is a controlled, deliberately measured answer, not a reflex-like discharge produced by affect. Cordelia explicitly frames the difficulty as putting her heart into public speech and then carefully qualifies her avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions as a principled boundary-setting response to Lear’s demand for competitive declarations. The qualification refuses exaggeration and rejects the premise that Cordelia should claim unlimited or exclusive love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while linking their answers to his bounty. After her sisters give expansive declarations, Cordelia answers Lear with a restrained avowal, defining her love by her filial bond rather than competing in hyperbole.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes her existing filial bond and associated duties to Lear, but the focal expression does not create a new prospective promise. Lear does not accept the answer as adequate: he tells her to amend it because it may damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare’s King Lear. The assessment is confined to the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly presents Cordelia as genuinely loving Lear, especially through her asides, but it cannot independently establish facts beyond this represented scene. The exact focal words are primarily an avowal; their obligation-invoking and boundary-setting forces depend on the immediately surrounding qualification and exchange.\"}"
